SIDE BY SIDE
==============================================================================
  step       TRITON (fla, hand-written)                           TINYGRAD
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  grid       grid = (NT, NC, B * HV)            # NT=T/64, NC=64/ 
  constexpr  BT = 64;  BC = 16;  NC = cdiv(BT, BC) = 4            
  load       b_k0  = tl.load(p_k0, mask=m_ck0).to(tl.float32)     
  load       b_g0  = tl.load(p_g0, mask=m_ck0).to(tl.float32)     
  load       b_gn1 = tl.load(g + ... + o_k, mask=m_k).to(tl.float 
  decay      b_gqn = tl.where(m_tc1[:, None], exp2(b_g1 - b_gn1[N 
  decay      b_kgt = tl.trans(b_k0 * exp2(b_gn1[None, :] - b_g0)) 
  mma        b_Akk10 = tl.dot(b_k1 * b_gqn, b_kgt, b_Akk10)       
  store      tl.store(p_Akk10, b_Akk10.to(dtype), mask=m_c1)      
             the [BC, BC] score block never leaves registers; the only
             extra buffer is Akkd = empty(B, T, HV, BC) -- [T,16], not [T,T]

  TinyGrad's equivalent of the three Triton lines is:
      decay = (gcum[:,:,:, :,None] - gcum[:,:,:,None,:]).exp()   -> [C,C,K]
      gram  = (decay * k[:,:,:,:,None] * k[:,:,:,None,:]).sum(-1) -> [C,C]
  Same result, but the intermediate is C x C x K instead of BC x K,
  and the contraction is a generic sum() rather than a tensor-core MMA.

